draft_id=replayssm-connector-validation
title="v2.5: let --use-replayssm coexist with OUR connector (the KV-connector clause of the RecoverSSM validator)"
upstream_issue="none (fork-local validation, v2.5 series patches 0071/0072/0085/0091)"
upstream_pr="none (fork-local)"

# ---- SAFETY VERDICT: DO NOT APPLY UNTIL THE QUESTION BELOW IS ANSWERED ------
# Below is what I read, then the one question, then what a YES and a NO each mean. A
# reader who wants to apply this should not be able to miss this section.

# ---- WHAT THE VALIDATION ACTUALLY IS (read from the installed tree) ---------
# `vllm/config/vllm.py::validate_mamba_cached_kernel`, the LAST clause of it:
#
#     if (self.kv_transfer_config is not None
#             and self.kv_transfer_config.is_kv_transfer_instance):
#         raise ValueError("--use-replayssm is incompatible with KV connectors "
#                           "(P/D disaggregation, KV cache offload)")
#
# `is_kv_transfer_instance` (`config/kv_transfer.py:143`) is
#     kv_connector is not None and kv_role in get_args(KVRole)
# with KVRole = {"kv_producer", "kv_consumer", "kv_both"}. Our launcher passes
# `kv_role: "kv_both"` (`serve-v2.2.sh:628`), so the clause FIRES FOR US. It is not a
# P/D-only guard in effect, whatever the parenthetical says.
#
# EVIDENCE OF CONSERVATISM, WHICH IS WHY THIS DRAFT EXISTS AT ALL:
#   * The clause sits OUTSIDE the `if self.cache_config.use_recoverssm:` branch, so it
#     also vetoes the plain ReplaySSM path - the one whose own commit message calls its
#     checkpoint READ-ONLY. A guard that catches a read-only path is not scoped to the
#     mechanism it names.
#   * It is not inside the architecture-specific block either: the validator takes care
#     to separate Qwen4Exp GDN/PLE from Kimi-K3 KDA and from the backends, then applies
#     this one check to everything.
#   * The fork's own reasoning is SILENT. Not one of the four RecoverSSM commit messages
#     (0071 share the kernels, 0072 "RecoverSSM for Qwen GDN and the PLE short conv",
#     0085 "use-replayssm without MTP falls back to the stock GDN path", 0091 pass
#     use_replayssm to the graph fallback) mentions a connector, an offload tier, or why
#     one would break. Compare 0085, which explains its own relaxation in full.

# ---- WHAT THE FORK SAYS RECOVERSSM DOES (0072's own words) ------------------
# "With --use-replayssm and speculative decoding, Gated DeltaNet layers verify the draft
#  window from a READ-ONLY CHECKPOINT and keep a PER-TOKEN REPLAY RECORD; after sampling
#  ONE COMMIT replays the accepted tokens and WRITES THE RECURRENT STATE ONCE. The PLE
#  short conv gets the same treatment."
# So per step, per live request row: a checkpoint read, a replay record written, and one
# deferred commit that writes the recurrent state. The validator's own PP comment names
# the machinery again: "Model Runner V2 defers the commit with a snapshot of its inputs
# (RecoverSSMState.detach_step) and masks freed rows".

# ---- THE FAILURE MODE THIS WOULD OPEN, STATED EXACTLY -----------------------
# Our connector restores host-RAM/disk bytes into GPU blocks inside the step
# (`start_load_kv`, which blocks the engine step - the settle window runs there too).
# RecoverSSM holds per-ROW scratch (the replay record and a deferred commit) whose
# authority is the state the step itself computed. If a load ever writes a recurrent-state
# row that RecoverSSM has a live deferred commit for, the two orderings differ only in
# which one wins the block - and BOTH orderings produce a plausible continuation, not an
# exception: either the commit overwrites the restored state with one derived from a
# checkpoint that predates the load, or the load overwrites a state the commit had
# already advanced. Nothing in either engine would report it. That is the incident class
# this repo has live evidence for, and it is why "it booted and answered" is not a test.

# ---- THE QUESTION, WHICH IS ANSWERABLE AND HAS NOT BEEN ANSWERED ------------
# CAN A CONNECTOR LOAD EVER TARGET A REQUEST ROW THAT RECOVERSSM HOLDS LIVE SCRATCH FOR?
# The shape that makes it impossible is the one our tier relies on today: a restore is
# admitted at SCHEDULING time for a request that has not run a decode step, so the row
# has no checkpoint, no replay record and no deferred commit. The shape that makes it
# possible is any path where a load lands on a row that is already live - a preempted and
# resumed request, a recompute after a failed load (`kv_load_failure_policy: recompute`,
# which our own launcher sets), or a second connector pass on an admitted request.
# Answer it by READING, not by reasoning: enumerate the call sites of `start_load_kv` and
# of the RecoverSSM commit in the installed tree, and ask whether any request is in both
# sets in one step. A test on a boot with BOTH flags and a `kvtier_disk_restore`-flagged
# request on a prompt with a planted code word - graded on greedy equality against the
# recomputed answer, never on `offers`/`adopted`/`reads_applied` - is the witness.

# ---- if the answer is NO ----------------------------------------------------
# Then the clause is a conservatism guard for our case and the patch is: move the clause
# inside a test that excludes OUR connector by NAME (leaving every foreign connector
# vetted exactly as the fork vetted them), keep every other clause untouched, and require
# the graded equality test above as the acceptance evidence. Re-measure the pool then:
# the 853,994 -> 924,993 distance is this draft's, and the 806,792 -> 853,994 step is
# DRAFT-0005's. Neither number is owed to either patch until a boot reports it.

# ---- which tree this entry describes ----------------------------------------
# v2.5.1 ONLY: `--use-replayssm`, RecoverSSM and `validate_mamba_cached_kernel`'s
# KV-connector clause are v2.5 concepts (fork patches 0071/0072/0085/0091). There is no
# v2.2 record to write, and inventing one would be a fabricated reference.
trees="v2.5.1"
tree_default=v2.5.1

