# ---- the v2.5.1 disk-tier substrate: CORRECTED, and how it was got wrong ------
# An earlier revision of this draft claimed the v2.5.1 port was blocked because "0004
# cannot be re-anchored onto `prefix_cacheable_group_ids` without porting 0001". That
# sentence was FALSE, and it came from a brief that was not checked against the bytes.
# Re-derived from the tracked patch files and the installed tree, 2026-10-22:
#   `grep -c prefix_cacheable patches/0004-kvtier-disk-restore/manager.py.patch` -> 0.
#     0004's store loop keys EVERY entry unconditionally off
#     `_store_key_pool.blocks[store_gpu[_i]].block_hash` and never consults a group set.
#     The name belongs to 0001, where it occurs 5 times.
#     (0004 has no metadata.py.patch at all - the metadata half is 0003's.)
#   `grep -c prefix_cacheable_group_ids\|prefix_cacheable_groups
#      vllm/v1/kv_cache_interface.py` on the installed v2.5.1 tree -> 3, with
#     `prefix_cacheable` properties at :165, :422, :455. The concept is NATIVE to
#     v2.5.1; only `manager.py` lacks it, because 0001 is not applied there.
#   The substrate itself has since LANDED: 0003 is applied to the v2.5.1 tree
#   (metadata.py +22, manager.py +32, `git apply --check` clean at a uniform -6 offset,
#   `py_compile` under the engine interpreter, an AST check proving the field is declared
#   AND passed to the constructor). Both trees' metadata.py post-images are byte-identical
#   at 4b4b8ddd..., which is 0003's flat `file_sha_post` AND its
#   `tree_v2_5_1_file_sha_post`. Inert until Boot 1; the engine is still pid 326471.
# So nothing here blocks DRAFT-0005, and this entry's own blocker is the open question
# below, not a missing substrate.

